-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Rust and JavaScript dependencies
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All Cargo and npm dependencies are used under permissive licences (MIT,
Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, Zlib, MPL-2.0 and similar). This is enforced
automatically: src-tauri/deny.toml declares the allowed set, and
scripts/check.sh and CI fail if a dependency outside it enters the tree. The npm packages compiled into the interface are listed, with
their licence, in THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt.

The full per-crate list, with versions and reproduced licence texts, is
generated by cargo-about into src-tauri/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt, which is
bundled into the installer (see the bundle.resources entry in
src-tauri/tauri.conf.json). To regenerate it after changing dependencies:

    cargo install cargo-about --features cli
    cargo about generate --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml --features ai \
        about.hbs -o src-tauri/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt

The cargo-about configuration is in src-tauri/about.toml; its accepted-licence
list mirrors the allow-list in deny.toml.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Dynamically linked components
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Components in this section are NOT compiled into the application binary. They
are distributed as separate shared libraries and loaded at runtime, which is
what their licences require and what keeps the application's own code separate
from them.

LibRaw
------

  Version:  0.22.2
  Licence:  GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1
            (full text in src-tauri/vendor/libraw/LICENSE.LGPL)
  Source:   https://www.libraw.org/download
            Windows: LibRaw-0.22.2-Win64.zip, which contains the complete
            corresponding source code for the binary shipped here.
            macOS: LibRaw-0.22.2.tar.gz, the official source release, from
            which the shipped libraw.dylib is built (see below).
  Version:  recorded in src-tauri/vendor/libraw/VERSION with the binaries'
            SHA-256; see docs/LIBRAW.md for how it is updated.
  Copyright: see src-tauri/vendor/libraw/COPYRIGHT

  LibRaw is **unmodified**; no patches of any kind have been applied. On
  Windows we ship the official binary distribution built by the LibRaw project.
  On macOS that distribution offers only static libraries, so we compile the
  official source release unchanged, with the LibRaw project's own makefile and
  compiler flags, into a shared library. scripts/vendor-libraw-macos.sh is the
  complete build procedure, and the result's SHA-256 is recorded beside it.

  LibRaw is **dynamically linked**, not compiled into this application. It is
  distributed as a separate shared library file (libraw.dll beside the program
  on Windows; libraw.dylib in the app's Contents/Frameworks on macOS) which the
  user is free to replace with another build of the same LibRaw version. Nothing
  in the application prevents this. On Windows the file is not signature-locked.
  On macOS the app bundle is code-signed as a whole, as macOS requires, so after
  replacing libraw.dylib the bundle must be re-signed locally, for example with
  `codesign --force --deep --sign - "SourceLight.app"`, which needs no
  certificate and which macOS permits for any application.

  This application uses LibRaw only to parse and decompress raw files. It does
  not call LibRaw's image-processing pipeline, and none of LibRaw's optional
  GPL-licensed demosaic packs are included in this distribution.

  Verification: the shared-library requirement is asserted in CI (see the
  "LibRaw is dynamically linked" step in .github/workflows/ci.yml). Static
  linking would silently void the licence terms above, so it is checked rather
  than assumed. The vendored package contains both libraw.lib (176 KB import
  library) and libraw_static.lib (4 MB static archive); build.rs links the
  former via `cargo:rustc-link-lib=dylib=libraw`. On macOS build.rs links
  libraw.dylib with `dylib=raw`, and `scripts/check-libraw.sh --link` confirms
  with `otool -L` that the binary imports it.

ONNX Runtime (DirectML build on Windows, CoreML build on macOS)
---------------------------------------------------------------

  Version:  1.22.0 (win-x64, DirectML execution provider)
            1.24.4 (osx-arm64, CoreML execution provider)
  Licence:  MIT
  Copyright: (c) Microsoft Corporation
  Source:   Windows: NuGet package Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.DirectML 1.22.0
            macOS: onnxruntime-osx-arm64-1.24.4.tgz from the GitHub release
            (https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime)
  Notices:  LICENSE.OnnxRuntime.txt and ThirdPartyNotices.OnnxRuntime.txt,
            shipped beside the executable (in Contents/Resources on macOS)

  ONNX Runtime executes the optional on-device AI models (subject/background
  cut-out, tagging, search, and the face/people features). It is used unmodified
  and loaded at runtime as a separate shared library (onnxruntime.dll;
  libonnxruntime.dylib in Contents/Frameworks on macOS). The AI
  build (`--features ai`) vendors it under src-tauri/vendor/onnxruntime/ and the
  bundler copies it beside the executable, where the loader finds it; 1.22.0 is
  the version the `ort` crate (=2.0.0-rc.10) targets, under its MIT licence.

  The DirectML build is used deliberately in place of the CUDA build: it gives
  GPU acceleration on any Direct3D 12 GPU without an NVIDIA-only dependency, from
  the ~16 MB onnxruntime.dll alone, with automatic CPU fallback.

  The AI models themselves are NOT distributed with this application, except
  YuNet and the two MediaPipe face models below, which the installer carries
  unmodified under their licences. The rest are downloaded when first
  needed from their original providers under each model's own licence, which the in-app model lists show. Only MIT
  and Apache-2.0 models are offered: anything research-only, non-commercial,
  or usable only after registering with its publisher is excluded, and a test
  enforces that. Every download is pinned to a fixed revision and checked
  against a SHA-256 recorded when its licence was checked (2026-09-24).

  On-device models (run by ONNX Runtime):

